findings.claim:
Blum's flap-drive patent describes the drive's motion logic:
- a reference run to learn end positions, which it detects when arm angular speed falls below a threshold;
- a motor switch-off angle offset before the end stop;
- collision monitoring by comparing speed/acceleration with a step profile recorded during the reference run, including collisions with 'the hand of a user';
- a rotary potentiometer for position;
- flap-type recognition from travel angle (fold-up about 120°, lift-up about 140°, swing-up about 130°).

findings.design_impact:
The drive itself detects obstruction against a learned profile, but an unattended automatic close five minutes later still risks pinching. Suggested safeguards:
- an audible or LED warning a few seconds before the closing press;
- optionally a presence sensor (PIR/ToF) to abort;
- after a press, if the sensor does not report 'closed' in time, assume the drive stopped on an obstruction: do not re-press repeatedly, raise an alert instead.

Also tell the owner that a reference run or reset (cross-link: manuals angle) will change drive behaviour.
